   "id": "a2-14",
   "name": "Classes of accounts and their codification"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Every account has a number (code). The digits show where it belongs:\n• the 1st digit is the class (for example 6 = expenses);\n• the first 2 digits are the main account (60 = purchases);\n• the first 3 digits are the divisional account (601 = purchases of goods);\n• more digits give sub-accounts (6011 = purchases of goods in the OHADA region).\nSo the longer the number, the more detailed the account.\nThe eight classes used in the accounts:\n1 durable resources; 2 fixed assets; 3 stocks; 4 third parties; 5 treasury; 6 expenses of ordinary activities; 7 income of ordinary activities; 8 other (extraordinary) expenses and income.\nExample: 571 Cash: class 5 (treasury), main account 57 (cash), account 571.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "c": 6011
  },
  "problem": "Explain the digits of account {c}.",
  "steps": [
   "The first digit, {6}, is the class: expenses of ordinary activities.",
   "{60} is the main account (purchases), {601} the divisional account (purchases of goods), and {6011} a sub-account."
  ],
  "answer": "Class {6}; main account {60}; divisional account {601}; sub-account {6011}.",
  "checks": []
 },
